Subject: Report namespace-scope function templates through `ParseCallbacks`

bindgen makes nothing of a function template declared at namespace scope, and
says nothing about it either. libclang reports one as a
`CXCursor_FunctionTemplate`; `FunctionSig::from_ty` declines that cursor, so
`Item::parse` falls through to the arm which files it among the cursor kinds
knowingly left unhandled. No item is made, no callback fires, and nothing in
the output distinguishes a scope which declares one from a scope which
declares none.

Generating nothing for one is right - a template has no monomorphization to
emit - but the silence costs a consumer which writes C++ of its own. A
function template participates in address-of overload resolution alongside
the ordinary functions of its name: `static_cast<int (*)(const M&)>(&pick)`
is legally satisfied by `template <class T> int pick(const T&)`, the
specialization deduced from the target type. A consumer which takes a
function's address that way - to pin a call to one declaration of an
overloaded name - has to know the name is also templated, because whether the
cast can quietly select the template instead turns on a declaration bindgen
never mentioned. `--report-member-function-templates` covers a class's
members; nothing covered the same declaration at namespace scope.

`--report-function-templates` reports each one through
`denote_function_template`: the name as C++ spells it, and how many template
parameters the declaration carries, counted as C++ declared them - deduction
and default template arguments may supply them, and a parameter pack counts
as the one parameter it is, libclang exposing no test for a pack. The report
is keyed by the module of the scope which declares the template, resolved
the way the parent of every item codegen announces is resolved, because a
consumer lines the two up by that id. That resolution has two steps neither
of which is the point of traversal. The scope is the semantic one, so an
out-of-line definition - `template <class T> int a::pick(const T&) { ... }`
after `a` has closed - reports under `a`, where C++ resolves the name, not
under the scope the file happens to write it in. And an inline namespace is
flattened to the nearest enclosing namespace which is not inline, as
codegen's announcements flatten everything an inline namespace holds: its
declarations shadow the enclosing scope's names, and its own module id is
one no announcement ever names.

The report is made during parsing, because there is nowhere later: no item
exists for codegen to walk. It is made before `Item::parse`'s redeclaration
shortcut rather than at the unhandled-kind arm, because a declaration whose
definition is out of line exits through that shortcut and never reaches the
arm. Two consequences a consumer must hold. Every declaration libclang
visits is reported, so a template declared and then defined arrives twice,
under the same module - and no allowlist has filtered anything: codegen's
module announcements, by contrast, cover only the modules the allowlist
keeps, so a report may name a module id nothing ever announces. Both are the
nature of a parse-time report rather than defects in it; deduplication is
one hash lookup on the consumer's side, and a scope which interests the
consumer is a scope whose module its other items already caused to be
announced.

`clang_getTemplateCursorKind` filters the cursor before it is reported: only
a template whose declaration would otherwise have been a plain function -
`CXCursor_FunctionDecl` - is one, which keeps a deduction guide out of the
report. A member function template never arrives here through a class's
body, `CompInfo::from_ty` visiting those children rather than `Item::parse`;
one defined out of line does arrive, and the same filter drops it, libclang
calling it a method template rather than a function template - the
declaration inside the class stays the member report's to make. A friend
function template is not reported either, hanging under a
`CXCursor_FriendDecl` nothing descends into.

No signature is reported, for the reason the member report gives: a function
template's signature may name the template's own parameters, which nothing
outside the template can write, and reporting the signatures which name none
of them would be a feature of its own.

What the report cannot see is a using-declaration: `using other::pick;` at
namespace scope is a `CXCursor_UsingDeclaration`, a different unhandled kind,
and the template it pulls into the scope is reported only in the namespace
which declares it. A consumer wanting the names visible in a scope, rather
than the names declared in it, has that much assembly left to do - and for
the part a using-declaration hides, no report to assemble it from.

Opt-in because keeping these is not free - a header which uses the standard
library declares a great many - and because a report made unconditionally
would be a new one for every consumer which already implements
`ParseCallbacks`. With the option off nothing changes at all.

